# The Gold-Standard Answer Key: 26 Felt-Need Topics → Best Bible Verses (World English Bible)

**TL;DR**

- This is a curated, hermeneutically vetted answer key mapping each of your 26 topics to a ranked list of the best Scripture passages, quoted in the public-domain World English Bible (WEB), each with a one-line theological rationale and contextual cautions where popular verses are commonly misapplied.
- Selections reflect genuine pastoral/theological consensus (GotQuestions, The Gospel Coalition, Desiring God, Ligonier/Tabletalk, Crossway, Cru, biblical-counseling resources) rather than mere popularity; where a beloved verse (Jeremiah 29:11, Philippians 4:13) is genuinely applicable but frequently abused, it is included WITH a caution.
- WEB is public domain (editor Michael Paul Johnson, derived from the 1901 ASV, published by eBible.org) and served by bible-api.com by default; the Classic WEB uses “Yahweh” in the Old Testament, does not capitalize deity pronouns, and modernizes archaic words including contractions like “Don’t.” A few verses have minor edition variants (flagged inline) — validate against your chosen WEB edition at build time.

**Key Findings**

- The strongest topical answers cluster around a repeating theological core: God’s presence (Isaiah 41:10; Psalm 23), the gospel (John 3:16; Romans 5:8; Ephesians 2:8-9), and God’s sovereignty in suffering (Romans 8:28, 38-39). These recur across many categories and should be weighted highly in any relevance-ranking algorithm.
- Several of the most-searched verses require contextual guardrails: Jeremiah 29:11 (written to exiled Judah, its promise tied to the end of Babylon’s 70-year captivity in v. 10 — not an individual earthly-prosperity guarantee), Philippians 4:13 (contentment in plenty and want, not achievement empowerment), and Proverbs 22:6 / Psalm 91 (proverbs and poetic promises are principles, not ironclad guarantees).
- For doctrine questions, theologians rely on a consistent set of proof texts (John 1:1; John 8:58; Colossians 2:9 for Christ’s deity; Matthew 28:19; 2 Corinthians 13:14 for the Trinity); eternal security is genuinely disputed between Reformed and Arminian traditions, so the answer key flags this rather than pretending consensus.

**Details**

## 1. Anxiety, Fear & Worry

1. **Philippians 4:6-7** — “In nothing be anxious, but in everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God.  And the peace of God, which surpasses all understanding, will guard your hearts and your thoughts in Christ Jesus.” — The central New Testament prescription: anxiety is answered by thankful, specific prayer, and God guards the mind.
1. **Isaiah 41:10** — “Don’t you be afraid, for I am with you. Don’t be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you. Yes, I will help you. Yes, I will uphold you with the right hand of my righteousness.” — Grounds fearlessness in God’s presence, help, and upholding.
1. **1 Peter 5:6-7** — “Humble yourselves therefore under the mighty hand of God, that he may exalt you in due time, casting all your worries on him, because he cares for you.” — Casting anxiety is an act of humility that trusts God’s care and timing.
1. **Matthew 6:31-34** — “Therefore don’t be anxious, saying, ‘What will we eat?’, ‘What will we drink?’ or, ‘With what will we be clothed?’ For the Gentiles seek after all these things; for your heavenly Father knows that you need all these things. But seek first God’s Kingdom and his righteousness; and all these things will be given to you as well. Therefore don’t be anxious for tomorrow, for tomorrow will be anxious for itself. Each day’s own evil is sufficient.” — Jesus reframes worry by pointing to the Father’s knowledge and priorities.
1. **John 14:27** — “Peace I leave with you. My peace I give to you; not as the world gives, give I to you. Don’t let your heart be troubled, neither let it be fearful.” — Christ’s own peace, distinct from the world’s, is the antidote to a troubled heart.
1. **2 Timothy 1:7** — “For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control.” — Fear is not the Spirit’s gift; power, love, and sound-mindedness are.
1. **Psalm 94:19** — “In the multitude of my thoughts within me, your comforts delight my soul.” — Names the racing-thought experience of anxiety and God’s consoling delight.
1. **Psalm 56:3** — “When I am afraid, I will put my trust in you.” — A short, memorizable turn from fear to trust.

## 2. Comfort, God’s Presence & Reassurance

1. **Psalm 23** — “Yahweh is my shepherd; I shall lack nothing. He makes me lie down in green pastures. He leads me beside still waters. He restores my soul. He guides me in the paths of righteousness for his name’s sake. Even though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil, for you are with me. Your rod and your staff, they comfort me. You prepare a table before me in the presence of my enemies. You anoint my head with oil. My cup runs over. Surely goodness and loving kindness shall follow me all the days of my life, and I will dwell in Yahweh’s house forever.” — The definitive psalm of God’s shepherding presence through every valley.
1. **2 Corinthians 1:3-4** — “Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of mercies and God of all comfort; who comforts us in all our affliction, that we may be able to comfort those who are in any affliction, through the comfort with which we ourselves are comforted by God.” — God as the source of all comfort, which then flows to others.
1. **Isaiah 43:2** — “When you pass through the waters, I will be with you, and through the rivers, they will not overflow you. When you walk through the fire, you will not be burned, and flame will not scorch you.” — God’s presence in (not around) the hardest passages.
1. **Deuteronomy 31:6** — “Be strong and courageous. Don’t be afraid or scared of them; for Yahweh your God himself is who goes with you. He will not fail you nor forsake you.” — The bedrock promise that God never abandons His people.
1. **Psalm 34:18** — “Yahweh is near to those who have a broken heart, and saves those who have a crushed spirit.” — God draws especially near to the brokenhearted.
1. **Matthew 28:20** — “teaching them to observe all things that I commanded you. Behold, I am with you always, even to the end of the age.” — Christ’s abiding presence with His people to the end.
1. **Psalm 46:1** — “God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble.” — God is not distant but a “very present” help.

## 3. Hope & the Future

1. **Romans 15:13** — “Now may the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace in believing, that you may abound in hope in the power of the Holy Spirit.” — Hope is a Spirit-empowered gift tied to believing.
1. **Romans 8:28** — “We know that all things work together for good for those who love God, for those who are called according to his purpose.” — God’s providence bends all circumstances toward good; caution: “good” is defined by v. 29 as conformity to Christ, not necessarily ease.
1. **Jeremiah 29:11** — “For I know the thoughts that I think toward you, says Yahweh, thoughts of peace, and not of evil, to give you hope and a future.” — A genuine window into God’s good purposes; CAUTION: spoken to Judah in Babylonian exile, its promise is explicitly tied to the end of Babylon’s 70-year captivity  (v. 10: “After seventy years are accomplished for Babylon, I will visit you and perform my good word toward you, in causing you to return to this place”). It is not an individual guarantee of earthly prosperity — apply it as evidence of God’s covenant character, ultimately secured in Christ.
1. **1 Peter 1:3-4** — “Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who according to his great mercy became our father again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead, to an incorruptible and undefiled inheritance that doesn’t fade away, reserved in Heaven for you.” — Grounds hope in the resurrection and a reserved inheritance.
1. **Lamentations 3:22-24** — “It is because of Yahweh’s loving kindnesses that we are not consumed, because his compassion doesn’t fail. They are new every morning; great is your faithfulness. ‘Yahweh is my portion,’ says my soul, ‘therefore I will hope in him.’” — Hope rooted in mercies that renew daily even amid ruin. (Edition note: some WEB printings render v. 22 “his mercies don’t fail.”)
1. **Romans 5:3-5** — “Not only this, but we also rejoice in our sufferings, knowing that suffering produces perseverance; and perseverance, proven character; and proven character, hope; and hope doesn’t disappoint us, because God’s love has been poured into our hearts through the Holy Spirit who was given to us.” — Suffering itself forges a hope that does not disappoint.
1. **Revelation 21:4** — “He will wipe away every tear from their eyes. Death will be no more; neither will there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain any more. The first things have passed away.” — The ultimate future hope: the end of all sorrow.

## 4. Strength & Endurance

1. **Isaiah 40:31** — “But those who wait for Yahweh will renew their strength. They will mount up with wings like eagles. They will run, and not be weary. They will walk, and not faint.” — Strength is renewed by waiting on the Lord.
1. **Philippians 4:13** — “I can do all things through Christ, who strengthens me.”  — Christ supplies strength for all circumstances; CAUTION: in context (4:11-12) this is contentment in both abundance and need, not a promise of achievement or success.
1. **2 Corinthians 12:9-10** — “He has said to me, ‘My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness.’ Most gladly therefore I will rather glory in my weaknesses, that the power of Christ may rest on me. Therefore I take pleasure in weaknesses, in injuries, in necessities, in persecutions, in distresses, for Christ’s sake. For when I am weak, then am I strong.” — God’s power is perfected precisely in our weakness.
1. **Joshua 1:9** — “Haven’t I commanded you? Be strong and courageous. Don’t be afraid. Don’t be dismayed, for Yahweh your God is with you wherever you go.” — Courage commanded on the basis of God’s presence.
1. **Isaiah 41:10** — “Don’t you be afraid, for I am with you. Don’t be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you. Yes, I will help you. Yes, I will uphold you with the right hand of my righteousness.” — God Himself strengthens and upholds.
1. **1 Corinthians 15:58** — “Therefore, my beloved brothers, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the Lord’s work, because you know that your labor is not in vain in the Lord.” — Endurance motivated by the resurrection’s guarantee that labor is not wasted.
1. **Galatians 6:9** — “Let’s not be weary in doing good, for we will reap in due season, if we don’t give up.” — Perseverance in well-doing with a promised harvest.
1. **Psalm 46:1** — “God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble.” — God is the source of strength in trouble.

## 5. Healing & Sickness

1. **James 5:14-15** — “Is any among you sick? Let him call for the elders of the assembly, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord; and the prayer of faith will heal him who is sick, and the Lord will raise him up. If he has committed sins, he will be forgiven.” — The New Testament’s direct instruction for the sick: call the elders, pray, anoint.
1. **Psalm 103:2-4** — “Praise Yahweh, my soul, and don’t forget all his benefits, who forgives all your sins, who heals all your diseases, who redeems your life from destruction, who crowns you with loving kindness and tender mercies.” — Healing is among God’s benefits, linked with forgiveness and redemption.
1. **Isaiah 53:5** — “But he was pierced for our transgressions. He was crushed for our iniquities. The punishment that brought our peace was on him; and by his wounds we are healed.” — Christ’s atoning wounds; CAUTION: the primary reference is healing from sin (cf. 1 Peter 2:24), though it grounds all healing in the cross.
1. **Psalm 147:3** — “He heals the broken in heart, and binds up their wounds.” — God’s healing extends to the emotionally and spiritually wounded.
1. **Jeremiah 17:14** — “Heal me, O Yahweh, and I will be healed. Save me, and I will be saved; for you are my praise.” — A model prayer for healing that acknowledges God as the healer.
1. **Matthew 11:28-30** — “Come to me, all you who labor and are heavily burdened, and I will give you rest. Take my yoke upon you and learn from me, for I am gentle and humble in heart; and you will find rest for your souls. For my yoke is easy, and my burden is light.” — Rest for the weary and burdened, including the sick in spirit.
1. **2 Corinthians 12:9** — “He has said to me, ‘My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness.’” — When physical healing is withheld, sufficient grace is promised (Paul’s “thorn”).

## 6. Grief, Loss & Death

1. **1 Thessalonians 4:13-14** — “But we don’t want you to be ignorant, brothers, concerning those who have fallen asleep, so that you don’t grieve like the rest, who have no hope. For if we believe that Jesus died and rose again, even so God will bring with Jesus those who have fallen asleep in him.” — The defining text: Christians grieve, but not as those without hope.
1. **John 11:25-26** — “Jesus said to her, ‘I am the resurrection and the life. He who believes in me will still live, even if he dies. Whoever lives and believes in me will never die. Do you believe this?’” — Jesus Himself is the answer to death.
1. **Revelation 21:4** — “He will wipe away every tear from their eyes. Death will be no more; neither will there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain any more. The first things have passed away.” — The final abolition of death and grief.
1. **Psalm 34:18** — “Yahweh is near to those who have a broken heart, and saves those who have a crushed spirit.” — God’s nearness to the grieving.
1. **Matthew 5:4** — “Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted.” — Jesus pronounces blessing and promised comfort on mourners.
1. **Psalm 23:4** — “Even though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil, for you are with me. Your rod and your staff, they comfort me.” — Comfort in the presence of God through death’s valley.
1. **2 Corinthians 1:3-4** — “Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of mercies and God of all comfort; who comforts us in all our affliction…” — God as the God of all comfort.
1. **John 14:1-3** — “Don’t let your heart be troubled. Believe in God. Believe also in me. In my Father’s house are many homes. If it weren’t so, I would have told you. I am going to prepare a place for you. If I go and prepare a place for you, I will come again, and will receive you to myself; that where I am, you may be there also.” — Jesus’ promise of a prepared place and reunion.

## 7. Depression, Loneliness & Despair

1. **Psalm 42:11** — “Why are you in despair, my soul? Why are you disturbed within me? Hope in God! For I shall still praise him, the saving help of my countenance, and my God.” — The psalmist preaches to his own downcast soul — a biblical-counseling model for depression.
1. **Psalm 34:18** — “Yahweh is near to those who have a broken heart, and saves those who have a crushed spirit.” — God’s special nearness to the crushed.
1. **Psalm 40:1-3** — “I waited patiently for Yahweh. He turned to me, and heard my cry. He brought me up also out of a horrible pit, out of the miry clay. He set my feet on a rock, and gave me a firm place to stand. He has put a new song in my mouth, even praise to our God. Many shall see it, and fear, and shall trust in Yahweh.” — Testimony of God lifting one out of the pit.
1. **Isaiah 41:10** — “Don’t you be afraid, for I am with you. Don’t be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you. Yes, I will help you. Yes, I will uphold you with the right hand of my righteousness.” — Reassurance against fear and dismay.
1. **Deuteronomy 31:8** — “Yahweh himself is who goes before you. He will be with you. He will not fail you nor forsake you. Don’t be afraid. Don’t be discouraged.” — God’s presence directly counters discouragement.
1. **Matthew 11:28-30** — “Come to me, all you who labor and are heavily burdened, and I will give you rest…” — Christ’s invitation to the weary and heavy-laden.
1. **Psalm 139:7-10** — “Where could I go from your Spirit? Or where could I flee from your presence? If I ascend up into heaven, you are there. If I make my bed in Sheol, behold, you are there! If I take the wings of the dawn, and settle in the uttermost parts of the sea, even there your hand will lead me, and your right hand will hold me.” — There is no place, even the depths, beyond God’s presence — an answer to loneliness.
1. **2 Corinthians 4:8-9** — “We are pressed on every side, yet not crushed; perplexed, yet not to despair; pursued, yet not forsaken; struck down, yet not destroyed.” — Honest about affliction, yet insistent that believers are never forsaken.

## 8. Love (God’s love & human love)

1. **John 3:16** — “For God so loved the world, that he gave his one and only Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish, but have eternal life.”   — The gospel in miniature: God’s love expressed in giving His Son.
1. **Romans 5:8** — “But God commends his own love toward us, in that while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us.” — God’s love proven at the cross while we were still sinners.
1. **1 John 4:7-10** — “Beloved, let’s love one another, for love is of God; and everyone who loves has been born of God, and knows God. He who doesn’t love doesn’t know God, for God is love… In this is love, not that we loved God, but that he loved us, and sent his Son as the atoning sacrifice for our sins.” — Defines love by God’s initiative and names God as love.
1. **Romans 8:38-39** — “For I am persuaded that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor things present, nor things to come, nor powers, nor height, nor depth, nor any other created thing, will be able to separate us from God’s love which is in Christ Jesus our Lord.” — The unbreakable security of God’s love.
1. **1 Corinthians 13:4-7** — “Love is patient and is kind. Love doesn’t envy. Love doesn’t brag, is not proud, doesn’t behave itself inappropriately, doesn’t seek its own way, is not provoked, takes no account of evil; doesn’t rejoice in unrighteousness, but rejoices with the truth; bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, endures all things.” — The definitive description of what love is and does.
1. **1 John 4:16-19** — “God is love, and he who remains in love remains in God, and God remains in him… There is no fear in love; but perfect love casts out fear, because fear has punishment. He who fears is not made perfect in love. We love him, because he first loved us.” — Our love is a response to being loved first.
1. **John 15:13** — “Greater love has no one than this, that someone lay down his life for his friends.” — The measure of the greatest love is self-sacrifice.
1. **Zephaniah 3:17** — “Yahweh, your God, is in the middle of you, a mighty one who will save. He will rejoice over you with joy. He will calm you in his love. He will rejoice over you with singing.” — God’s delight and joy over His people. (Edition note: some WEB printings read “in the midst of you”   and “rest in his love” / “quiet you.”)  

## 9. Forgiveness (receiving & giving)

1. **1 John 1:9** — “If we confess our sins, he is faithful and righteous to forgive us the sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.” — The promise for receiving forgiveness: confess and be cleansed.
1. **Ephesians 4:32** — “And be kind to one another, tender hearted, forgiving each other, just as God also in Christ forgave you.” — The pattern for giving forgiveness: as God forgave you.
1. **Colossians 3:13** — “bearing with one another, and forgiving each other, if any man has a complaint against any; even as Christ forgave you, so you also do.” — Forgiveness modeled on Christ’s forgiveness of us.
1. **Matthew 6:14-15** — “For if you forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you. But if you don’t forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses.” — Jesus links our forgiving others to our own forgiveness.
1. **Psalm 103:10-12** — “He has not dealt with us according to our sins, nor repaid us for our iniquities. For as the heavens are high above the earth, so great is his loving kindness toward those who fear him. As far as the east is from the west, so far has he removed our transgressions from us.” — The completeness of God’s forgiveness.
1. **Matthew 18:21-22** — “Then Peter came and said to him, ‘Lord, how often shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? Until seven times?’ Jesus said to him, ‘I don’t tell you until seven times, but, until seventy times seven.’” — Forgiveness is to be unlimited.
1. **Micah 7:18-19** — “Who is a God like you, who pardons iniquity, and passes over the disobedience of the remnant of his heritage? He doesn’t retain his anger forever, because he delights in loving kindness. He will again have compassion on us. He will tread our iniquities under foot; and you will cast all their sins into the depths of the sea.” — God’s delight in pardoning sin.
1. **Isaiah 1:18** — “‘Come now, and let’s reason together,’ says Yahweh: ‘Though your sins are as scarlet, they shall be as white as snow. Though they are red like crimson, they shall be as wool.’” — God’s offer to cleanse the deepest stains.

## 10. Marriage, Dating & Relationships

1. **Genesis 2:24** — “Therefore a man will leave his father and his mother, and will join with his wife, and they will be one flesh.” — The foundational definition of marriage as a one-flesh covenant union (affirmed by Jesus in Matthew 19).
1. **Ephesians 5:25** — “Husbands, love your wives, even as Christ also loved the assembly, and gave himself up for it.” — The husband’s self-sacrificial love modeled on Christ.
1. **1 Corinthians 13:4-7** — “Love is patient and is kind. Love doesn’t envy. Love doesn’t brag, is not proud…” — The character of love essential to any marriage.
1. **Ecclesiastes 4:9-12** — “Two are better than one, because they have a good reward for their labor. For if they fall, the one will lift up his fellow; but woe to him who is alone when he falls, and doesn’t have another to lift him up… A threefold cord is not quickly broken.” — The strength of committed partnership.
1. **Mark 10:9** — “What therefore God has joined together, let no man separate.” — The permanence of the marriage bond.
1. **Colossians 3:18-19** — “Wives, be in subjection to your husbands, as is fitting in the Lord. Husbands, love your wives, and don’t be bitter against them.” — Complementary responsibilities in marriage.
1. **1 Peter 3:7** — “You husbands, in the same way, live with your wives according to knowledge, giving honor to the woman, as to the weaker vessel, as being also joint heirs of the grace of life, that your prayers may not be hindered.” — Honoring one’s spouse as a co-heir, with prayer at stake.
1. **2 Corinthians 6:14** — “Don’t be unequally yoked with unbelievers, for what fellowship have righteousness and iniquity? Or what fellowship has light with darkness?” — Wisdom for dating/choosing a partner; CAUTION: primary context is broader partnerships, but widely and reasonably applied to marriage.

## 11. Parenting, Children & Family

1. **Proverbs 22:6** — “Train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will not depart from it.” — The classic parenting proverb; CAUTION: a proverb (general principle of wise probability), not an ironclad guarantee of outcome.
1. **Deuteronomy 6:6-7** — “These words, which I command you today, shall be on your heart; and you shall teach them diligently to your children, and shall talk of them when you sit in your house, and when you walk by the way, and when you lie down, and when you rise up.” — Faith is passed on through everyday, immersive instruction.
1. **Ephesians 6:1-4** — “Children, obey your parents in the Lord, for this is right. ‘Honor your father and mother,’ which is the first commandment with a promise… You fathers, don’t provoke your children to wrath, but nurture them in the discipline and instruction of the Lord.” — Balanced instruction to children and parents.
1. **Psalm 127:3-5** — “Behold, children are a heritage of Yahweh. The fruit of the womb is his reward. As arrows in the hand of a mighty man, so are the children of youth. Happy is the man who has his quiver full of them.” — Children are a gift and reward from God.
1. **Proverbs 29:17** — “Correct your son, and he will give you peace; yes, he will bring delight to your soul.” — The value of loving discipline.
1. **Joshua 24:15** — “If it seems evil to you to serve Yahweh, choose today whom you will serve… but as for me and my house, we will serve Yahweh.” — A family’s decisive commitment to serve the Lord.
1. **Colossians 3:21** — “Fathers, don’t provoke your children, so that they won’t be discouraged.” — A caution against exasperating discipline.

## 12. Money, Finances, Work & Provision

1. **Matthew 6:31-33** — “Therefore don’t be anxious, saying, ‘What will we eat?’, ‘What will we drink?’ or, ‘With what will we be clothed?’… But seek first God’s Kingdom and his righteousness; and all these things will be given to you as well.” — Kingdom priorities over financial anxiety.
1. **Philippians 4:19** — “My God will supply every need of yours according to his riches in glory in Christ Jesus.”  — God’s promise to supply needs; CAUTION: “need,” not every want, and spoken to generous partners in the gospel.
1. **1 Timothy 6:6-10** — “But godliness with contentment is great gain… But those who are determined to be rich fall into a temptation, a snare, and many foolish and harmful lusts… For the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil. Some have been led astray from the faith in their greed, and have pierced themselves through with many sorrows.” — The heart-danger of money and the gain of contentment.
1. **Hebrews 13:5** — “Be free from the love of money, content with such things as you have, for he has said, ‘I will in no way leave you, neither will I in any way forsake you.’” — Contentment grounded in God’s abiding presence.
1. **Proverbs 3:9-10** — “Honor Yahweh with your substance, with the first fruits of all your increase; so your barns will be filled with plenty, and your vats will overflow with new wine.” — Honoring God with the firstfruits.
1. **Matthew 6:24** — “No one can serve two masters, for either he will hate the one and love the other, or else he will be devoted to one and despise the other. You can’t serve both God and Mammon.” — The impossibility of dividing ultimate loyalty between God and money.
1. **Colossians 3:23-24** — “And whatever you do, work heartily, as for the Lord, and not for men, knowing that from the Lord you will receive the reward of the inheritance; for you serve the Lord Christ.” — Work as service to Christ.
1. **2 Corinthians 9:6-8** — “He who sows sparingly will also reap sparingly. He who sows bountifully will also reap bountifully. Let each man give according as he has determined in his heart, not grudgingly or under compulsion, for God loves a cheerful giver. And God is able to make all grace abound to you, that you, always having all sufficiency in everything, may abound to every good work.” — The principle of cheerful, generous giving.

## 13. Guidance, Decisions & God’s Will

1. **Proverbs 3:5-6** — “Trust in Yahweh with all your heart, and don’t lean on your own understanding. In all your ways acknowledge him, and he will make your paths straight.” — The premier guidance text: trust and acknowledge God, not self.
1. **James 1:5** — “But if any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives to all liberally and without reproach; and it will be given to him.” — God gives wisdom generously to those who ask.
1. **Psalm 32:8** — “I will instruct you and teach you in the way which you shall go. I will counsel you with my eye on you.” — God’s personal commitment to guide.
1. **Romans 12:2** — “Don’t be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your mind, so that you may prove what is the good, well-pleasing, and perfect will of God.” — God’s will is discerned by a transformed mind.
1. **Proverbs 16:9** — “A man’s heart plans his course, but Yahweh directs his steps.” — Human planning under God’s sovereign direction.
1. **Psalm 119:105** — “Your word is a lamp to my feet, and a light for my path.” — Scripture as the primary means of guidance.
1. **Isaiah 30:21** — “and when you turn to the right hand, and when you turn to the left, your ears will hear a voice behind you, saying, ‘This is the way. Walk in it.’” — God’s promise to direct the way.
1. **John 16:13** — “However when he, the Spirit of truth, has come, he will guide you into all truth, for he will not speak from himself; but whatever he hears, he will speak. He will declare to you things that are coming.” — The Spirit’s role in guiding into truth.

## 14. Faith, Doubt & Trust

1. **Hebrews 11:1** — “Now faith is assurance of things hoped for, proof of things not seen.” — The Bible’s own definition of faith.
1. **Hebrews 11:6** — “Without faith it is impossible to be well pleasing to him, for he who comes to God must believe that he exists, and that he is a rewarder of those who seek him.” — Faith is essential and God rewards those who seek Him.
1. **Mark 9:24** — “Immediately the father of the child cried out with tears, ‘I believe. Help my unbelief!’” — The honest prayer of one who believes yet struggles with doubt.
1. **Proverbs 3:5-6** — “Trust in Yahweh with all your heart, and don’t lean on your own understanding. In all your ways acknowledge him, and he will make your paths straight.” — Trust that does not depend on our own understanding.
