## 24. Theology & Doctrine Questions

**Is Jesus God?**

1. **John 1:1** — “In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God.”  — The Word (Christ) is both distinct from and identified as God.
1. **John 1:14** — “The Word became flesh, and lived among us. We saw his glory, such glory as of the one and only Son of the Father, full of grace and truth.”  — The eternal Word became incarnate.
1. **John 8:58** — “Jesus said to them, ‘Most certainly, I tell you, before Abraham came into existence, I AM.’”  — Jesus applies the divine name (I AM, cf. Exodus 3:14) to Himself.
1. **John 20:28** — “Thomas answered him, ‘My Lord and my God!’” — Thomas’s confession of Jesus as God, uncorrected by Jesus.
1. **Colossians 2:9** — “For in him all the fullness of the Godhead dwells bodily.”  — The fullness of deity dwells in Christ.
1. **Titus 2:13** — “looking for the blessed hope and appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ.” — Jesus called “our great God and Savior.”

**The Trinity:**
7. **Matthew 28:19** — “Go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.” — One “name,” three persons.
8. **2 Corinthians 13:14** — “The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, God’s love, and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit, be with you all. Amen.”  — The three persons in one benediction.
9. **Matthew 3:16-17** — “Jesus, when he was baptized, went up directly from the water: and behold, the heavens were opened to him. He saw the Spirit of God descending as a dove, and coming on him. Behold, a voice out of the heavens said, ‘This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased.’” — All three persons present at Jesus’ baptism.

**What happens after death?**
10. **2 Corinthians 5:8** — “We are courageous, I say, and are willing rather to be absent from the body and to be at home with the Lord.” — For the believer, to be absent from the body is to be with the Lord.
11. **Luke 23:43** — “Jesus said to him, ‘Assuredly I tell you, today you will be with me in Paradise.’” — Immediate presence with Christ after death.
12. **Hebrews 9:27** — “Inasmuch as it is appointed for men to die once, and after this, judgment.” — One death, then judgment (against reincarnation).
13. **Philippians 1:23** — “But I am hard pressed between the two, having the desire to depart and be with Christ, which is far better.” — Death for the believer means being with Christ.

**Can you lose your salvation?**
14. **John 10:28-29** — “I give eternal life to them. They will never perish, and no one will snatch them out of my hand. My Father who has given them to me is greater than all. No one is able to snatch them out of my Father’s hand.” — The strongest text for eternal security.
15. **Philippians 1:6** — “being confident of this very thing, that he who began a good work in you will complete it until the day of Jesus Christ.” — God finishes the work He begins.
16. **Romans 8:38-39** — “For I am persuaded that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor things present, nor things to come, nor powers, nor height, nor depth, nor any other created thing, will be able to separate us from God’s love which is in Christ Jesus our Lord.” — Nothing separates believers from God’s love. NOTE: This is a genuine intramural debate — Reformed traditions cite these texts for eternal security; Arminian/Wesleyan traditions cite warning passages (Hebrews 6:4-6; 10:26-29;  John 15:6) for the possibility of apostasy. Your engine should present both streams honestly rather than adjudicate silently.

**Why does God allow evil/suffering?**
17. **Genesis 50:20** — “As for you, you meant evil against me, but God meant it for good, to save many people alive, as is happening today.” — God sovereignly works even human evil for good.
18. **Romans 8:28** — “We know that all things work together for good for those who love God, for those who are called according to his purpose.” — God’s redemptive purpose in all things.
19. **Revelation 21:4** — “He will wipe away every tear from their eyes. Death will be no more; neither will there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain any more. The first things have passed away.” — The final resolution of evil and suffering.

