**Recommendations**

- **Adopt this as your test-suite gold standard, but store two data fields per verse:** the exact WEB text AND a “contextual caution” flag, so your engine can surface the caution when a commonly-misapplied verse (Jeremiah 29:11, Philippians 4:13, Proverbs 22:6, Psalm 91:1-4, Mark 11:24) is returned. This lets the engine be both pastorally warm and hermeneutically honest.
- **Verify every verse’s exact WEB wording programmatically at build time** against bible-api.com (which serves the WEB by default; note its rate limit of 15 requests per 30 seconds per IP  and that its JSON “text” field wraps content in newlines). A few passages have minor WEB edition variants — standardize on whichever edition your API returns: Lamentations 3:22 (“compassion doesn’t fail” vs. “mercies don’t fail”), Zephaniah 3:17 (“in the middle of you / in the midst of you,” “calm you / rest in / quiet you”), Psalm 23:1 (“I shall lack nothing” vs. “I shall not want”), and Jeremiah 29:11 ending.
- **For the eternal-security question (Category 24), present BOTH the security proof-texts and the Arminian warning passages** (Hebrews 6:4-6; 10:26-29; John 15:6), since this is a genuine, unresolved intramural debate. Do not let the engine adjudicate it silently.
- **Staged rollout:** (1) Ship categories 1-23 first — they enjoy the broadest cross-tradition consensus and lowest theological risk. (2) Ship category 24 (doctrine) only after adding a short “traditions differ” note where relevant. (3) A/B test category rankings against real user queries.
- **Benchmarks/thresholds that would change these selections:** If user-testing shows searchers on emotional topics (categories 1-7) prefer Psalms over epistles, promote more psalms into the top 3. If click-through on doctrine answers is low, pair each proof text with a one-sentence catechism-style answer and cross-references. If any caution-flagged verse generates confusion, move the caution into the primary display rather than a tooltip.

**Caveats**

- Verse text is the World English Bible (public domain; editor Michael Paul Johnson, derived from the 1901 ASV, published by eBible.org).  The Classic WEB uses “Yahweh” and does not capitalize deity pronouns; other WEB sub-editions substitute “the LORD.” A handful of passages have minor revision differences (flagged above); confirm against your chosen WEB edition at build time before locking the test suite.
- Rankings reflect a Protestant, evangelical, broadly Reformed-friendly consensus (the sources you named: Piper/Desiring God, Keller/TGC, Spurgeon, GotQuestions, Ligonier, CCEF, Crossway, Cru). Roman Catholic, Orthodox, or strongly Arminian/Wesleyan users would weight some passages differently — especially on salvation’s security, the sacraments/ordinances, and Marian/deuterocanonical material (not represented here).
- “Best” verses were chosen for contextual fidelity, not merely popularity; where popularity and sound context conflict, cautions are included so the engine surfaces the truly fitting answer without discarding the verse a searcher expects.
- Because live web-search and direct verse-API fetching were constrained in this build, the distinctive/edge-case WEB wordings (Psalm 23, Isaiah 41:10, Zephaniah 3:17, Jeremiah 29:10-11, John 3:16) were cross-checked against WEB sources, but you should still run the full list through bible-api.com once programmatically before publishing to guarantee byte-exact wording across all ~180 references.